{id:"A",label:"Variant A · Heat Map",listening:{script:`Host: Professor Malik, your student team mapped heat across the city last summer. What did you learn? Professor Malik: First, that a heat map can easily become a map of where your volunteers happen to be. We initially had dozens of readings from the university district and almost none from industrial streets or older housing areas. So before collecting more data, we redesigned the sampling plan. Students followed fixed routes at the same three times of day and carried identical shielded sensors. Shade mattered enormously. A tree-lined street could be several degrees cooler than an exposed square only two blocks away. But the most interesting result was about night-time heat. Some densely built areas cooled very slowly after sunset, even when their daytime peak was not the highest. That matters because the body needs relief overnight. We worked with community groups to compare our maps with reports from residents, but we were careful: a temperature map does not tell you who is medically vulnerable. The city is now using the data to choose locations for temporary shade structures and drinking-water points. I am pleased, but I also tell students that a useful map is the beginning of a decision, not the decision itself.`,questions:[
{text:"What flaw affected the team's first collection?",options:["Sensors were all broken","Readings reflected volunteer locations more than the whole city","Temperatures were recorded only at night","Industrial streets were over-sampled"],answer:"b",explanation:"Sampling was geographically biased."},
{text:"How did the team improve the study?",options:["They used fixed routes and consistent times","They collected only university data","They removed all sensors","They asked residents to estimate temperatures"],answer:"a",explanation:"Standardised routes and timing improved comparability."},
{text:"Why were sensors shielded?",options:["To keep measurement conditions more consistent","To hide them from residents","To make them heavier","To record sound"],answer:"a",explanation:"Shielding reduces unwanted environmental influence on temperature readings."},
{text:"What local factor strongly changed street temperature?",options:["Tree shade","Shop prices","Traffic signs","Building names"],answer:"a",explanation:"Tree-lined streets were notably cooler."},
{text:"What surprised the team about some dense areas?",options:["They cooled slowly at night","They were always coolest at noon","They had no residents","Their daytime peaks were always highest"],answer:"a",explanation:"Slow night-time cooling was the notable pattern."},
{text:"Why is night-time cooling important?",options:["The body needs recovery from heat","Sensors only work after dark","Traffic stops","Trees grow faster"],answer:"a",explanation:"Professor Malik links night cooling to physiological relief."},
{text:"What did community groups contribute?",options:["Reports from residents","New weather satellites","Medical diagnoses","City budgets"],answer:"a",explanation:"Maps were compared with residents' experiences."},
{text:"What limitation does Professor Malik stress?",options:["A temperature map cannot identify individual medical vulnerability","Temperature cannot be measured outdoors","Community reports are useless","Shade has no effect"],answer:"a",explanation:"Environmental exposure and individual vulnerability are different."},
{text:"How is the city using the findings?",options:["To locate temporary shade and water points","To close all roads","To remove trees","To replace hospitals"],answer:"a",explanation:"Those targeted interventions are mentioned."},
{text:"What is Professor Malik's final warning?",options:["Maps should inform decisions rather than replace them","Only professionals should see maps","Data is never useful","Every hot area needs the same solution"],answer:"a",explanation:"He distinguishes evidence from the final policy choice."}]},reading:{passage:`When Maps Look More Certain Than the Data

Maps are persuasive. A coloured surface can make scattered measurements look like a complete picture, even when large areas contain little or no data. This is especially important in environmental mapping, where measurements may be collected by volunteers, mobile sensors or short field campaigns.

To create a continuous heat map, software often estimates values between measured points. This process, called interpolation, is useful, but it is still an estimate. If sensors are clustered in parks and commercial centres, the map may appear detailed while representing residential streets poorly.

Design choices also affect interpretation. A map using deep red for temperatures above 30°C may feel more alarming than one using pale colours, even if both show the same data. The boundaries chosen for categories matter too: a street at 29.9°C and one at 30.1°C may be shown in dramatically different colours despite being practically similar.

Responsible maps therefore communicate uncertainty. They may show where measurements are dense or sparse, allow users to view individual data points, or explain how estimates were calculated. Some projects publish several maps based on different assumptions rather than one apparently definitive image.

Yet uncertainty does not make a map useless. Decision-makers rarely receive perfect information. A well-designed map can identify areas that deserve further investigation or immediate low-risk action, such as adding temporary shade. The danger lies in confusing visual confidence with scientific certainty.

Good mapping is therefore partly an ethical task. The cartographer decides what is visible, what is hidden and how strongly a pattern appears. A map does not merely report evidence; it frames it.`,questions:[
{text:"Why can maps appear more complete than their evidence?",options:["Colours can fill spaces between limited measurements","Sensors always cover every street","Maps cannot show points","Environmental data is exact"],answer:"a",explanation:"Interpolation can visually create continuity from sparse measurements."},
{text:"What is interpolation?",options:["Estimating values between measured locations","Deleting unusual readings","Changing units","Interviewing residents"],answer:"a",explanation:"That is the definition given."},
{text:"Why is clustered sampling a problem?",options:["Some areas may be represented poorly","It makes every sensor inaccurate","It prevents any colour use","It guarantees false values"],answer:"a",explanation:"Dense data in one area can hide gaps elsewhere."},
{text:"How can colour choice influence readers?",options:["It can make the same data feel more or less alarming","It changes actual temperature","It removes categories","It improves sensor precision"],answer:"a",explanation:"Visual intensity shapes perception."},
{text:"What issue arises from category boundaries?",options:["Very similar values may look dramatically different","All streets become identical","Maps become impossible to print","Measurements disappear"],answer:"a",explanation:"Thresholds can exaggerate small numerical differences."},
{text:"How can maps communicate uncertainty?",options:["Show sampling density and methods","Remove all legends","Use one strong colour","Hide data points"],answer:"a",explanation:"Transparency about density and methods is recommended."},
{text:"Why might a project publish several maps?",options:["To show how assumptions affect the result","To confuse readers","To avoid collecting data","To increase file size"],answer:"a",explanation:"Alternative assumptions reveal uncertainty."},
{text:"Does uncertainty make maps useless?",options:["No, they can still guide investigation or low-risk action","Yes, always","Only if colours are used","Only for governments"],answer:"a",explanation:"The writer explicitly rejects that conclusion."},
{text:"What is the main danger identified?",options:["Mistaking visual confidence for scientific certainty","Using temporary shade","Displaying data points","Explaining methods"],answer:"a",explanation:"This phrase summarises the central warning."},
{text:"Why does the writer call mapping partly ethical?",options:["Mapmakers choose what is visible and how patterns are framed","Maps are sold for money","Sensors require permission","All maps affect elections"],answer:"a",explanation:"Representation choices carry responsibility."}]},uoe:[
{text:"The first map was biased ___ areas where volunteers lived and studied.",options:["towards","from","for","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Biased towards is the correct collocation."},
{text:"Had the team not changed its routes, some districts ___ underrepresented.",options:["would remain","would have remained","remain","had remained now"],answer:"b",explanation:"Third conditional result."},
{text:"The researchers took great care ___ overstate what the map showed.",options:["not to","to not having","for not","not"],answer:"a",explanation:"Take care not to do something."},
{text:"Night-time heat turned ___ to be as important as daytime peaks.",options:["out","up","down","off"],answer:"a",explanation:"Turn out to be is fixed."},
{text:"The map is intended to inform decisions, not to stand ___ expert judgement.",options:["in for","up at","out on","by with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Stand in for means substitute."},
{text:"Sparse data can give rise ___ false confidence.",options:["to","for","at","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Give rise to is the fixed expression."},
{text:"No sooner ___ the first map been published than residents pointed out gaps.",options:["had","has","was","did"],answer:"a",explanation:"No sooner had ... than ... requires inversion."},
{text:"The project is one of several studies ___ aim is to guide urban adaptation.",options:["whose","which","who","whom"],answer:"a",explanation:"Whose expresses possession for studies."},
{text:"The city would rather ___ temporary shade first than make an irreversible change.",options:["test","to test","testing","tested"],answer:"a",explanation:"Would rather takes the bare infinitive."},
{text:"Even a technically accurate map may be open ___ misinterpretation.",options:["to","for","with","at"],answer:"a",explanation:"Open to is the correct collocation."}] ,writing:{text:"Write a proposal for your town or school explaining how environmental data could be used to improve one public space. Discuss what data would be needed, how uncertainty should be communicated and one low-risk action that could be tested first.",min:180,max:250,requirements:["Use a proposal structure","Name a specific environmental problem","Explain what evidence should be collected","Discuss uncertainty or sampling limitations","Recommend a reversible first action"]}},
{id:"B",label:"Variant B · Archive Voices",listening:{script:`Interviewer: Dr Ward, your archive is using AI transcription on old oral-history recordings. Does it save time? Dr Ward: Enormously, but only if we treat the transcript as a draft. Our recordings include regional accents, overlapping speech and names that no modern language model is likely to know. The software may turn a family surname into an ordinary word or confidently invent punctuation that changes the meaning. We therefore keep the audio as the primary source. Volunteers correct transcripts while listening, and uncertain words are marked rather than guessed. The technology has still transformed access. Before, a researcher might need to listen to forty hours of audio to find one reference. Now a rough text search can point them to five likely minutes. We also publish short audio clips beside transcripts so readers can hear tone and hesitation. Another issue is privacy. Some interviews were recorded decades ago under agreements that never imagined online publication. Faster transcription does not automatically give us permission to publish everything. Technology can reduce the labour of finding words, but the archive remains responsible for context, consent and accuracy.`,questions:[
{text:"How does Dr Ward describe AI transcripts?",options:["Final authoritative texts","Drafts that require checking","More reliable than audio","Unnecessary for research"],answer:"b",explanation:"The transcript is explicitly treated as a draft."},
{text:"Why are old recordings difficult to transcribe automatically?",options:["They contain accents, overlap and unfamiliar names","They are all silent","They use only modern studio speech","They have no people speaking"],answer:"a",explanation:"Those features challenge automatic recognition."},
{text:"What punctuation problem is mentioned?",options:["Confident punctuation can alter meaning","Punctuation is never generated","Only commas are missing","Names become punctuation marks"],answer:"a",explanation:"Inserted punctuation can change interpretation."},
{text:"What remains the primary source?",options:["The audio recording","The AI transcript","A volunteer summary","A website search result"],answer:"a",explanation:"The archive retains audio as authoritative."},
{text:"What should volunteers do with uncertain words?",options:["Mark uncertainty instead of guessing","Delete the whole interview","Choose the most common word","Ask AI to invent one"],answer:"a",explanation:"Uncertainty is documented, not concealed."},
{text:"What major access benefit does transcription provide?",options:["It helps locate relevant moments quickly","It improves microphone quality","It creates new interviews","It removes the need for audio"],answer:"a",explanation:"Text search narrows long recordings to likely segments."},
{text:"Why are audio clips published beside transcripts?",options:["To let readers hear tone and hesitation","To advertise equipment","To replace all text","To shorten interviews"],answer:"a",explanation:"Vocal features can matter to interpretation."},
{text:"What privacy issue arises?",options:["Old consent agreements may not cover online publication","Volunteers dislike computers","Recordings contain no names","Search is too fast"],answer:"a",explanation:"Historical consent may be limited."},
{text:"Does faster transcription create permission to publish?",options:["No","Always","Only for famous people","Only after one year"],answer:"a",explanation:"Technical ability and permission are separate."},
{text:"What responsibility remains with the archive?",options:["Context, consent and accuracy","Only software speed","Selling recordings","Replacing volunteers"],answer:"a",explanation:"Those three responsibilities form the conclusion."}]},reading:{passage:`A Transcript Is Not the Voice

Written transcripts are invaluable. They can be searched, quoted and read quickly. For people who cannot hear an audio recording, they may provide essential access. Yet a transcript is not simply speech converted into text.

Spoken language contains pauses, emphasis, laughter, interruption and uncertainty. Consider the sentence "I never said she stole it." Stressing a different word can change the implication without changing any letters. A plain transcript may not capture that.

Editors therefore make choices. Should a long pause be marked? Should repeated words remain? Should "um" and "you know" be removed for readability? In a legal interview, hesitation may matter greatly. In a public exhibition, excessive detail may make the text difficult to read.

Automatic transcription adds another layer. Modern systems can be impressively fast, but errors are not random. Unfamiliar names, minority accents and noisy recordings may be misrecognised more often. If users assume machine-generated text is neutral, these patterns can become invisible.

Good archives respond by stating how transcripts were produced, linking text to audio where possible and marking uncertain passages. Some keep two versions: a close transcript for researchers and an edited reading version for general visitors.

The question is not whether transcripts are good or bad. It is what purpose a transcript serves and what information is lost or changed in creating it. Treating text as a representation rather than a perfect copy makes both technology and editorial judgement easier to evaluate.`,questions:[
{text:"What advantage of transcripts is mentioned first?",options:["They can be searched and read quickly","They preserve every vocal detail","They remove privacy issues","They never contain errors"],answer:"a",explanation:"Searchability and speed are explicit benefits."},
{text:"Why is the example 'I never said she stole it' used?",options:["To show stress can change implication","To teach grammar","To prove transcripts are illegal","To show speech is always ambiguous"],answer:"a",explanation:"Identical words can carry different emphasis."},
{text:"What editorial choice is discussed?",options:["Whether to mark pauses and repeated words","Whether to change the interviewee","Whether to invent names","Whether to remove every sentence"],answer:"a",explanation:"Transcription conventions require judgement."},
{text:"Why might detailed hesitation be preserved in a legal interview?",options:["It may be significant evidence","It makes the text shorter","It improves spelling","It entertains readers"],answer:"a",explanation:"Context determines the importance of speech features."},
{text:"What risk comes with automatic transcription?",options:["Errors may affect some accents and names more often","It always works slowly","It cannot search text","It removes audio files"],answer:"a",explanation:"Error distribution may be uneven."},
{text:"What assumption does the writer warn against?",options:["That machine-generated text is neutral","That audio contains speech","That readers value access","That archives use computers"],answer:"a",explanation:"Automation can hide patterned errors."},
{text:"What transparency practice is recommended?",options:["Explain how transcripts were produced","Delete uncertain words silently","Publish text without audio","Hide editing rules"],answer:"a",explanation:"Method disclosure helps users evaluate the representation."},
{text:"Why might an archive keep two transcript versions?",options:["Different audiences have different needs","One version is always false","Audio cannot be stored","Researchers dislike detail"],answer:"a",explanation:"Research precision and public readability may require different formats."},
{text:"What central question does the article propose?",options:["What purpose the transcript serves and what changes in creating it","Whether all speech should become text","Which software is cheapest","How fast people can type"],answer:"a",explanation:"Purpose and transformation are the final focus."},
{text:"What overall view does the writer take?",options:["Transcripts are useful representations, not perfect copies","Transcripts should replace audio","Automatic systems should never be used","Only legal transcripts matter"],answer:"a",explanation:"The conclusion is balanced and purpose-sensitive."}]},uoe:[
{text:"The software is capable ___ producing a rough transcript in minutes.",options:["of","to","for","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Capable of is fixed."},
{text:"The archive insists ___ checking names against other records.",options:["on","in","at","to"],answer:"a",explanation:"Insist on + gerund."},
{text:"Were the audio to be lost, some nuances ___ impossible to recover.",options:["would be","will be","are","had been"],answer:"a",explanation:"Inverted hypothetical conditional."},
{text:"Editors must account ___ the needs of different audiences.",options:["for","with","at","to"],answer:"a",explanation:"Account for means consider/explain."},
{text:"The system sometimes makes ___ a word when it cannot recognise a name.",options:["up","out","off","over"],answer:"a",explanation:"Make up means invent."},
{text:"The transcript should not be regarded ___ a perfect copy of speech.",options:["as","like to","for","by"],answer:"a",explanation:"Regard as is fixed."},
{text:"Only after listening again ___ the volunteer notice the error.",options:["did","had","was","has"],answer:"a",explanation:"Negative/fronted adverbial triggers inversion."},
{text:"The archive has introduced guidelines, ___ purpose is to make uncertainty visible.",options:["whose","which","who","whom"],answer:"a",explanation:"Whose can refer to guidelines and expresses possession."},
{text:"The more sensitive the material, the more carefully access decisions need ___.",options:["making","to be made","make","made them"],answer:"b",explanation:"Passive infinitive is required."},
{text:"The new workflow has cut ___ the time needed to locate references.",options:["down on","out from","up with","off to"],answer:"a",explanation:"Cut down on means reduce."}] ,writing:{text:"Write an essay discussing whether AI-generated transcripts should be treated as reliable sources in education, journalism or historical research. Consider benefits, limitations and the role of human checking.",min:180,max:250,requirements:["Use an argumentative structure","Present at least one benefit and two limitations","Discuss human responsibility","Use at least two examples of cautious/qualified language","Finish with a balanced conclusion"]}},
{id:"C",label:"Variant C · Explain It Well",listening:{script:`Host: Final-year student Maya won a science communication contest, but she says her first draft was terrible. Maya: I tried to prove I knew the subject, so every sentence contained technical vocabulary. My mentor asked me to explain the same idea to a twelve-year-old without losing the important uncertainty. That second part was harder than replacing jargon. Scientists often say things like "the evidence suggests" because results are limited. In my first simple version, I accidentally changed that to "scientists know". It sounded clearer but was less accurate. I eventually used an analogy: I compared our climate model to planning a journey with several possible routes. The analogy helped, but I also said where it breaks down. A model is not a road map, and probability is not the same as choosing a route. During the contest, the judges asked an unexpected question. Instead of pretending I knew, I said what evidence I would need to answer it. Later one judge told me that response showed more understanding than a confident guess. Good communication, I learned, is not making science sound certain. It is making both the knowledge and its limits understandable.`,questions:[
{text:"What was wrong with Maya's first draft?",options:["It used technical language to display expertise","It was too short","It contained no science","It used only pictures"],answer:"a",explanation:"She prioritised sounding knowledgeable over clarity."},
{text:"What extra challenge did her mentor set?",options:["Keep important uncertainty while simplifying","Remove every scientific term","Make the talk longer","Avoid younger audiences"],answer:"a",explanation:"Accuracy of uncertainty had to survive simplification."},
{text:"How did Maya distort one claim in her simple version?",options:["She changed 'evidence suggests' into 'scientists know'","She removed a date","She added a false scientist","She changed the topic"],answer:"a",explanation:"The wording became more certain than the evidence."},
{text:"What analogy did she eventually use?",options:["A journey with several possible routes","A broken machine","A theatre performance","A recipe"],answer:"a",explanation:"She compared modelling to route planning."},
{text:"What did Maya do with the analogy?",options:["She also explained where it failed","She presented it as exact","She removed all caveats","She used it only in writing"],answer:"a",explanation:"She marked the limits of the comparison."},
{text:"What distinction did she make?",options:["Probability is not the same as choosing a route","Models are literal road maps","Every route has equal probability","Climate is a journey"],answer:"a",explanation:"She explicitly warns against taking the analogy literally."},
{text:"What happened during judges' questions?",options:["She was asked something she could not answer confidently","She forgot her entire talk","No questions were asked","She changed subjects"],answer:"a",explanation:"An unexpected question tested her response."},
{text:"How did she answer?",options:["She described what evidence she would need","She invented a confident answer","She refused to speak","She asked the audience"],answer:"a",explanation:"She responded methodologically rather than guessing."},
{text:"Why did a judge value that answer?",options:["It demonstrated understanding and intellectual honesty","It was the shortest answer","It used more jargon","It avoided science"],answer:"a",explanation:"Knowing how to find out is evidence of understanding."},
{text:"What is Maya's definition of good science communication?",options:["Making knowledge and limits understandable","Making science sound certain","Avoiding all analogies","Using as many technical words as possible"],answer:"a",explanation:"Her final sentence states this directly."}]},reading:{passage:`The Hidden Cost of a Good Analogy

Analogies are powerful because they connect unfamiliar ideas to familiar experiences. Electricity can be described as water flowing through pipes; memory as a filing system; the immune system as an army. These comparisons can help a learner build an initial mental model.

The problem is that an analogy highlights some features and hides others. Water in a pipe physically travels from one place to another, while electrical behaviour in a circuit is more complicated. An immune system does defend the body, but immune cells do not have intentions or a central commander in the way an army might.

A successful analogy therefore has two jobs. It should create a useful connection, and it should make its own limits visible. Teachers sometimes do this by asking students: "Where does this comparison stop working?" That question turns a potential weakness into a learning opportunity.

Analogies can also affect public debate. Calling data "the new oil" emphasizes economic value but may encourage people to think of data as a natural resource waiting to be extracted. Calling the brain a "computer" can suggest that thinking is primarily information processing and underplay emotion, biology and social context.

None of this means analogies should be abandoned. Technical explanations without them can be inaccessible, and every explanation selects some features over others. The aim is to use analogies consciously.

One useful habit is to offer two different analogies for the same concept. If both illuminate different aspects, learners are less likely to mistake either one for a literal description. The best analogy is not the one that disappears into the explanation; it is the one whose usefulness and boundaries are both understood.`,questions:[
{text:"Why are analogies useful initially?",options:["They connect unfamiliar ideas to familiar experiences","They make every concept exact","They remove the need for evidence","They replace definitions permanently"],answer:"a",explanation:"Familiar structure supports early understanding."},
{text:"What is the main limitation of an analogy?",options:["It emphasizes some features while hiding others","It is always too technical","It cannot be remembered","It only works in science"],answer:"a",explanation:"Selective similarity is the central risk."},
{text:"Why is the army analogy for immunity imperfect?",options:["Immune cells do not literally have intentions or one commander","Armies are smaller than cells","The body has no defence","Immune cells never move"],answer:"a",explanation:"Human intention and hierarchy do not map literally."},
{text:"What two jobs should a successful analogy perform?",options:["Create a connection and reveal its limits","Entertain and simplify every fact","Replace evidence and theory","Use two technical terms"],answer:"a",explanation:"Usefulness and boundaries should both be explicit."},
{text:"Why ask 'Where does this comparison stop working?'",options:["To turn limitations into learning","To show analogies are useless","To test spelling","To end discussion"],answer:"a",explanation:"Examining breakdown deepens understanding."},
{text:"What may 'data is the new oil' encourage?",options:["A resource-extraction way of thinking","A literal belief that data is liquid","Less economic interest","Better privacy automatically"],answer:"a",explanation:"The metaphor frames data as extractable resource."},
{text:"What can the brain-computer analogy underplay?",options:["Emotion, biology and social context","Memory","Information processing","Electricity"],answer:"a",explanation:"Those dimensions may be obscured."},
{text:"Does the writer recommend abandoning analogies?",options:["No","Yes, in all cases","Only in education","Only in public debate"],answer:"a",explanation:"The writer explicitly defends careful use."},
{text:"Why might two analogies be better than one?",options:["They reduce the risk of treating one model as literal","They always agree completely","They make explanations shorter","They eliminate uncertainty"],answer:"a",explanation:"Different comparisons reveal different aspects."},
{text:"What defines the 'best' analogy in the final sentence?",options:["Its usefulness and boundaries are understood","It becomes invisible","It sounds most dramatic","It has no limitations"],answer:"a",explanation:"The conclusion values conscious use."}]},uoe:[
{text:"Maya managed to get the idea ___ without oversimplifying it.",options:["across","over with","through to","away"],answer:"a",explanation:"Get an idea across means communicate it successfully."},
{text:"Her mentor objected ___ presenting a tentative result as certain.",options:["to","against","at","for"],answer:"a",explanation:"Object to + gerund."},
{text:"The analogy was useful, provided that its limits ___ clear.",options:["were made","made","will make","making"],answer:"a",explanation:"Passive past/subjunctive-like structure fits the condition."},
{text:"Had Maya guessed, she ___ less credible to the judges.",options:["would have seemed","would seem yesterday","seemed now","had seemed"],answer:"a",explanation:"Third conditional result."},
{text:"Scientific uncertainty is often difficult to put ___ plain language.",options:["into","onto","at","for"],answer:"a",explanation:"Put into plain language is fixed."},
{text:"So convincing ___ the analogy that several students remembered it weeks later.",options:["was","it was","did","has"],answer:"a",explanation:"Fronted so + adjective triggers inversion."},
{text:"The speaker acknowledged the point ___ which the comparison broke down.",options:["at","on","to","for"],answer:"a",explanation:"At which is the correct relative phrase."},
{text:"Clear communication does not necessarily come ___ the expense of accuracy.",options:["at","with","by","from"],answer:"a",explanation:"At the expense of is fixed."},
{text:"The judges praised her for ___ uncertainty rather than hiding it.",options:["acknowledging","acknowledge","to acknowledge","acknowledged"],answer:"a",explanation:"For is followed by -ing."},
{text:"What matters is not how simple an explanation sounds, but how faithfully it ___ the evidence.",options:["represents","is representing always","represent","has represent"],answer:"a",explanation:"Singular subject 'it' takes represents."}] ,writing:{text:"Write an article for an education magazine on whether analogies and metaphors help or mislead students when learning difficult ideas. Use examples and explain how teachers can make the limits of comparisons clear.",min:180,max:250,requirements:["Add a headline","Give at least two examples","Discuss both benefit and risk","Explain how to mark the limits of an analogy","Finish with a practical recommendation"]}},
{id:"D",label:"Variant D · Research Credit",listening:{script:`Host: Dr Chen, your laboratory recently changed the way student contributions are credited. Why? Dr Chen: We had a familiar problem. A research paper lists authors, but a project may involve many different contributions: designing software, recruiting participants, maintaining equipment, visualising data, or checking reproducibility. Junior researchers sometimes did substantial work that was almost invisible because they were not the person who wrote most of the paper. We now use a contribution statement with fourteen categories. Before a project begins, team members discuss likely roles, and we revisit the statement before publication because contributions change. This has not removed disagreement. In fact, it sometimes reveals it earlier. One student believed that collecting hundreds of samples guaranteed authorship; another supervisor thought data collection alone should be acknowledged but not necessarily count as authorship. Instead of relying on assumptions, the team had to discuss intellectual responsibility, interpretation and accountability. The system is not a scoring table. Five categories do not automatically make someone a first author. Its value is that hidden labour becomes discussable. Transparency does not solve every power imbalance, but silence usually protects the people who already have more power.`,questions:[
{text:"What problem motivated the change?",options:["Many contributions were invisible in traditional author lists","Papers had too many pages","Software was banned","Students refused to collect data"],answer:"a",explanation:"Contribution diversity was not captured well by names alone."},
{text:"Which contribution is mentioned?",options:["Maintaining equipment","Selling journals","Designing uniforms","Booking travel only"],answer:"a",explanation:"Equipment maintenance is one example."},
{text:"Who could be disadvantaged by the old system?",options:["Junior researchers doing substantial but less visible work","Only journal editors","Participants only","Readers"],answer:"a",explanation:"Invisible labour often fell to junior team members."},
{text:"What does the new system use?",options:["A contribution statement with fourteen categories","A secret ranking","Only alphabetical order","A points competition"],answer:"a",explanation:"The lab uses a structured contribution taxonomy."},
{text:"When are roles first discussed?",options:["Before the project begins","After publication","Only if conflict occurs","At graduation"],answer:"a",explanation:"Early discussion is deliberate."},
{text:"Why is the statement revisited?",options:["Roles can change during a project","The categories expire weekly","Journals require daily updates","Students forget names"],answer:"a",explanation:"Actual contribution may differ from plans."},
{text:"Did the system eliminate disagreement?",options:["No, it sometimes exposed it sooner","Yes, completely","Only for supervisors","Only after publication"],answer:"a",explanation:"Transparency can surface conflict rather than erase it."},
{text:"What disagreement is described?",options:["Whether large-scale data collection automatically guarantees authorship","Whether papers need titles","Whether software counts as equipment","Whether participants need consent"],answer:"a",explanation:"Authorship threshold was contested."},
{text:"Why is the system not a scoring table?",options:["Number of categories does not mechanically determine author order","There are no categories","Only supervisors receive credit","Scores are secret"],answer:"a",explanation:"Qualitative responsibility matters."},
{text:"What is Dr Chen's broader argument?",options:["Making labour visible helps address power, even if it cannot solve everything","Transparency removes all inequality","Authorship should be random","Only writing matters"],answer:"a",explanation:"Visibility creates space for fairer discussion."}]},reading:{passage:`Who Gets Credit for Collaborative Work?

School and university projects often end with a simple list of names. The list tells us who participated, but little about what each person actually did. In small groups this may not matter. In larger research, creative or technical projects, it can become a serious issue.

Traditional authorship grew from fields in which one or a few people could plausibly design, conduct and write a study. Modern projects may involve programmers, statisticians, fieldworkers, technicians and community partners. Deciding who deserves authorship can therefore become contested.

One response is contributorship: describing specific roles instead of relying only on author order. A paper might state who designed the method, curated data, wrote software, created visualisations or drafted the text. This gives readers more information and can make hidden labour visible.

However, transparency does not automatically produce fairness. A senior researcher may still receive prestigious authorship for limited work, while a junior colleague performs many tasks with less influence over the final interpretation. Contribution statements describe work; they do not by themselves redistribute power.

There are also questions about responsibility. If twenty people contributed, who is accountable for an error? Some journals ask authors to identify which contributors can take responsibility for particular parts of the work and which senior authors guarantee the integrity of the whole project.

For student group work, the lesson is useful. Fair credit is not only about dividing tasks equally. It requires discussing expectations early, recording contributions honestly and deciding who is responsible for final decisions. A list of names is easy; a fair account of collaboration is harder.`,questions:[
{text:"What is the weakness of a simple name list?",options:["It says little about individual contributions","It is too long","It cannot include students","It is always unfair"],answer:"a",explanation:"Participation is visible, roles are not."},
{text:"Why has traditional authorship become more complicated?",options:["Modern projects involve many specialised roles","People write fewer papers","Names are harder to spell","Projects are always smaller"],answer:"a",explanation:"Division of labour has expanded."},
{text:"What is contributorship?",options:["Describing specific roles performed by each contributor","Removing all author names","Ranking people by age","Letting software choose authors"],answer:"a",explanation:"It records kinds of work."},
{text:"What benefit does contributorship offer?",options:["It can reveal hidden labour","It guarantees first authorship","It eliminates errors","It shortens projects"],answer:"a",explanation:"Visibility is a major advantage."},
{text:"Why doesn't transparency guarantee fairness?",options:["Power differences can remain even when tasks are documented","Roles cannot be written down","Senior people never contribute","Junior people always agree"],answer:"a",explanation:"Description and power redistribution are distinct."},
{text:"What example of unfairness is given?",options:["A senior researcher may receive prestige for limited work","A programmer always becomes first author","Fieldworkers own the journal","Every contributor gets equal authority"],answer:"a",explanation:"Status can still shape credit."},
{text:"What responsibility issue arises in large teams?",options:["Who is accountable for mistakes","Who chooses the font","Who attends lunch","Who owns the building"],answer:"a",explanation:"Accountability can become diffuse."},
{text:"How do some journals address responsibility?",options:["They identify responsibility for parts and overall integrity","They ban teams","They remove methods","They publish anonymously only"],answer:"a",explanation:"Explicit guarantor roles are mentioned."},
{text:"What lesson is applied to student group work?",options:["Expectations and responsibility should be discussed early","Tasks must always be equal","Only the final writer deserves credit","Names should be random"],answer:"a",explanation:"Process clarity is recommended."},
{text:"What contrast closes the article?",options:["Listing names is easy; fairly describing collaboration is harder","Collaboration is easier than solo work","Research is easier than schoolwork","Errors are easier than success"],answer:"a",explanation:"The final sentence contrasts simple listing with fair accounting."}]},uoe:[
{text:"The new policy is intended to shed light ___ hidden contributions.",options:["on","at","for","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Shed light on is fixed."},
{text:"The team agreed ___ authorship criteria before data collection.",options:["on","to with","at","for"],answer:"a",explanation:"Agree on a topic/criterion."},
{text:"Had expectations been discussed earlier, the dispute ___ less severe.",options:["might have been","might be yesterday","was","had"],answer:"a",explanation:"Third conditional possibility."},
{text:"The statement makes it harder for substantial work to go ___.",options:["unacknowledged","acknowledge","acknowledging","to acknowledge"],answer:"a",explanation:"The adjective unacknowledged fits."},
{text:"No contribution category, taken ___ itself, determines author order.",options:["by","with","at","from"],answer:"a",explanation:"By itself means alone."},
{text:"The supervisor was reluctant to give ___ control over the final interpretation.",options:["up","out","off","through"],answer:"a",explanation:"Give up control is the correct phrase."},
{text:"Not until the final meeting ___ the team realise how differently members understood authorship.",options:["did","had","was","has"],answer:"a",explanation:"Not until at the front triggers inversion."},
{text:"The policy requires contributors to state the work for ___ they are responsible.",options:["which","who","whose","what"],answer:"a",explanation:"Preposition + which refers to work."},
{text:"Transparency is valuable, but it is no substitute ___ fair decision-making.",options:["for","to","of","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Substitute for is fixed."},
{text:"The revised system has made disagreements easier to bring ___ into the open.",options:["out","up to","off","away"],answer:"a",explanation:"Bring out into the open means make visible/public."}] ,writing:{text:"Write an essay on whether group projects should require a formal record of each student's contribution. Discuss fairness, teamwork, accountability and possible disadvantages.",min:180,max:250,requirements:["Use an argumentative structure","Discuss both advantages and disadvantages","Include accountability as a separate point","Use at least two advanced linking expressions","Give a clear conclusion"]}},
{id:"E",label:"Variant E · Moving Better",listening:{script:`Host: Transport designer Elena Ruiz has been working with students on a safer route to school. Elena: The original request was for a new cycle lane, but we resisted jumping straight to a solution. We first mapped how students actually travelled. Some cycled, but many walked part of the route after getting off a bus. The most dangerous location was not the busy main road; it was a quiet side street where drivers turned quickly because they did not expect pedestrians. We installed temporary planters that narrowed the entrance and painted a wider pedestrian area for six weeks. Car speeds fell, but delivery drivers complained that the turning space was too tight. Instead of removing the experiment, we moved two planters and marked a loading bay twenty metres away. The second version kept the lower speeds without blocking deliveries. What mattered was treating complaints as data, not as proof that the whole idea had failed. Student volunteers also counted near-misses, not just accidents, because serious accidents are fortunately rare and would take years to compare. The final design will still need formal approval, but the trial gave the city better evidence about trade-offs.`,questions:[
{text:"What solution was originally requested?",options:["A new cycle lane","A railway station","A bus ban","A car park"],answer:"a",explanation:"That was the starting request."},
{text:"Why didn't the team immediately build the requested solution?",options:["They wanted to understand actual travel patterns first","They had no paint","Cycling was illegal","Students refused to participate"],answer:"a",explanation:"Diagnosis came before intervention."},
{text:"Where was the most dangerous location?",options:["A quiet side street with fast turning vehicles","The busiest main road","Inside the school gate","At the bus station"],answer:"a",explanation:"Risk came from unexpected pedestrian movement on a side street."},
{text:"What temporary change was tested?",options:["Planters narrowed the entrance and pedestrian space was widened","The street was permanently closed","A bridge was built","Traffic lights were removed"],answer:"a",explanation:"The intervention was deliberately reversible."},
{text:"What positive effect occurred?",options:["Car speeds fell","Deliveries stopped","Cycling disappeared","Bus fares dropped"],answer:"a",explanation:"Lower speeds were measured."},
{text:"What problem did delivery drivers report?",options:["Turning space became too tight","The road was too wide","There were no signs","Students blocked the bus"],answer:"a",explanation:"The first design constrained deliveries."},
{text:"How was the design adjusted?",options:["Two planters were moved and a loading bay marked nearby","All planters were removed","The experiment was cancelled","A second school was closed"],answer:"a",explanation:"A compromise preserved speed reduction."},
{text:"How does Elena say complaints should be treated?",options:["As data about trade-offs","As proof the project failed","As irrelevant","As legal decisions"],answer:"a",explanation:"Feedback informs iteration."},
{text:"Why count near-misses?",options:["Serious accidents are too rare for quick comparison","Near-misses are easier to invent","Accidents cannot be recorded","Students prefer smaller numbers"],answer:"a",explanation:"Near-misses provide more frequent safety evidence."},
{text:"What did the trial ultimately provide?",options:["Better evidence about trade-offs","Automatic final approval","A perfect design","No further decisions"],answer:"a",explanation:"The trial informs, but does not replace, approval."}]},reading:{passage:`The Trouble with Measuring Road Safety

A road becomes safer and no one crashes. How do you prove the improvement worked?

Serious collisions are relatively rare at any single location. That is fortunate, but it creates a statistical problem. If a junction had one collision last year and none this year, the difference may reflect chance rather than a safer design. Waiting for many years would provide more data, but communities often need decisions sooner.

Transport researchers therefore use additional indicators. Vehicle speed is one. The faster a vehicle is moving, the less time a driver has to react and the more severe a collision is likely to be. Another indicator is a "near-miss": an event in which people avoid a collision only through sudden braking, stopping or changing direction.

Near-miss data is useful but difficult. One observer may describe an interaction as dangerous while another sees normal traffic behaviour. Video analysis can improve consistency, but it raises privacy questions and requires clear definitions.

Researchers may also study whether people change their behaviour. If a new crossing is technically safer but pedestrians avoid it because it feels inconvenient, the design may not achieve its purpose. Conversely, an increase in walking or cycling may be a positive outcome even if traffic numbers stay the same.

No single measure captures road safety completely. Good evaluation combines collision records, speed, behaviour, user experience and near-misses. The aim is not to find one perfect number but to build a convincing pattern from different kinds of evidence.`,questions:[
{text:"Why are serious collisions difficult to use as the only short-term measure?",options:["They are relatively rare at one location","They cannot be recorded","They are always caused by weather","They happen every day"],answer:"a",explanation:"Low frequency makes year-to-year comparison unstable."},
{text:"Why might one year with zero collisions prove little?",options:["Random variation may explain the change","Zero is not a number","Roads are never safer","Records are secret"],answer:"a",explanation:"A small count is highly affected by chance."},
{text:"What is one reason speed matters?",options:["Higher speed reduces reaction time and can increase severity","Slower vehicles make roads longer","Speed has no relation to safety","Only cyclists have speed"],answer:"a",explanation:"Both reaction and impact severity are mentioned."},
{text:"What is a near-miss?",options:["A dangerous interaction where collision is narrowly avoided","A minor collision","A traffic fine","A late bus"],answer:"a",explanation:"It involves evasive action without actual impact."},
{text:"What weakness does near-miss data have?",options:["Observers may judge events differently","It always requires ten years","It cannot involve pedestrians","It is identical to collision data"],answer:"a",explanation:"Subjective classification is a challenge."},
{text:"What new concern can video analysis create?",options:["Privacy","Road width","Fuel cost","Weather"],answer:"a",explanation:"Recording people requires privacy safeguards."},
{text:"Why study behaviour after a redesign?",options:["A technically safe feature may still be avoided by users","People never change routes","Behaviour replaces speed data","Only cyclists matter"],answer:"a",explanation:"Use determines whether a design achieves its purpose."},
{text:"What could increased walking or cycling indicate?",options:["A positive outcome even without traffic-volume change","A failed project","More collisions automatically","Lower privacy"],answer:"a",explanation:"Mode uptake can be a success indicator."},
{text:"What does the writer recommend combining?",options:["Several types of safety evidence","Only collision totals","Only user opinions","Only video"],answer:"a",explanation:"Multi-measure evaluation is the central recommendation."},
{text:"What is the final goal of evaluation?",options:["Build a convincing pattern from different evidence","Find one perfect number","Avoid all uncertainty","Delay every decision for years"],answer:"a",explanation:"The conclusion rejects reliance on a single metric."}]},uoe:[
{text:"The team refrained ___ committing to a permanent solution too early.",options:["from","to","at","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Refrain from + gerund."},
{text:"The first design gave rise ___ complaints from delivery drivers.",options:["to","for","at","with"],answer:"a",explanation:"Give rise to is fixed."},
{text:"Were the trial permanent, changes ___ much more expensive.",options:["would be","will be","are","had been"],answer:"a",explanation:"Inverted second conditional."},
{text:"The researchers factored user behaviour ___ their evaluation.",options:["into","on","at","for"],answer:"a",explanation:"Factor into means include as a consideration."},
{text:"A rare event is difficult to draw firm conclusions ___.",options:["from","with","to","by"],answer:"a",explanation:"Draw conclusions from evidence."},
{text:"The revised layout struck a better balance ___ safety and deliveries.",options:["between","among of","with","to"],answer:"a",explanation:"Balance between X and Y."},
{text:"Only after the planters were moved ___ the complaints decrease.",options:["did","had","were","have"],answer:"a",explanation:"Only after triggers inversion."},
{text:"The team used indicators, some of ___ were based on observed behaviour.",options:["which","who","whose","what"],answer:"a",explanation:"Some of which refers to indicators."},
{text:"The trial was not meant to do away ___ formal approval.",options:["with","from","at","by"],answer:"a",explanation:"Do away with means eliminate."},
{text:"Evidence from several sources is more convincing than relying ___ a single measure.",options:["on","in","to","by"],answer:"a",explanation:"Rely on is fixed."}] ,writing:{text:"Write a proposal for improving safety on a route used by students. Explain how you would identify the real problem, test a temporary solution and evaluate it using more than one kind of evidence.",min:180,max:250,requirements:["Use a proposal structure","Describe the location and risk","Explain one temporary/reversible intervention","Use at least three evaluation measures","Discuss one trade-off or possible objection"]}}
